WEBVTT

00:04.662 --> 00:07.137
This lesson is about the workplace exam.

00:07.137 --> 00:10.513
Every shift, before miners start work in a place,

00:10.513 --> 00:13.275
a competent person looks that place over for anything

00:13.275 --> 00:15.763
that could hurt someone. It's one of the most important

00:15.763 --> 00:18.288
safety checks at a mine, and it's also one of the

00:18.288 --> 00:21.012
standards inspectors look at most closely.

00:22.153 --> 00:24.703
Part 48 says your training covers the health and

00:24.703 --> 00:26.815
safety side of the tasks you'll do,

00:26.815 --> 00:29.878
and how to recognize hazards. The workplace exam

00:29.878 --> 00:32.665
is where hazard recognition happens every single

00:32.665 --> 00:35.190
day. Even though you won't be the examiner as a

00:35.190 --> 00:37.727
new miner, you'll work in places that were just

00:37.727 --> 00:41.053
examined, and you'll read and rely on that exam.

00:42.199 --> 00:44.687
Here's what the federal standard actually says.

00:44.687 --> 00:48.012
A competent person, designated by the mine operator,

00:48.012 --> 00:50.724
examines each working place at least once each

00:50.724 --> 00:54.062
shift, before miners begin work in that place.

00:54.062 --> 00:56.324
The exam looks for conditions that may adversely

00:56.324 --> 00:59.424
affect safety or health. That covers far more than

00:59.424 --> 01:02.074
rock. It means anything that could hurt you or

01:02.074 --> 01:03.199
make you sick.

01:04.320 --> 01:06.945
A competent person is someone who has the abilities

01:06.945 --> 01:09.657
and experience to do that inspection well.

01:09.657 --> 01:12.507
The operator names who that is. At our sites,

01:12.507 --> 01:15.220
supervisors document hazards on daily inspection

01:15.220 --> 01:17.820
sheets, and those sheets go to Hunter Kenney.

01:17.820 --> 01:20.095
Your supervisor will tell you who does the exam

01:20.095 --> 01:22.633
on your shift and where the record is kept.

01:23.754 --> 01:26.641
So what's a working place? It's any place in or

01:26.641 --> 01:29.416
around the mine where work is being performed.

01:29.416 --> 01:31.916
At our operations that means the underground headings

01:31.916 --> 01:35.091
and faces, the pit and benches, haul roads and

01:35.091 --> 01:38.516
dump points, the crushers, screens and conveyors,

01:38.516 --> 01:41.029
the shop, the stockpiles and loadout,

01:41.029 --> 01:42.267
and the scale area.

01:43.380 --> 01:45.418
If people are going to work there this shift,

01:45.418 --> 01:47.505
it gets examined before they start.

01:47.505 --> 01:49.855
A place nobody is working in that shift doesn't

01:49.855 --> 01:52.767
need an exam. But the moment a crew is sent there,

01:52.767 --> 01:55.618
the exam comes first. If you're sent somewhere

01:55.618 --> 01:57.517
and you're not sure it was examined,

01:57.517 --> 01:59.130
ask before you start work.

02:02.446 --> 02:04.496
Let's walk a working place the way an examiner

02:04.496 --> 02:07.596
does. Ground comes first. Underground,

02:07.596 --> 02:10.571
that means the back, the ribs, the face and the

02:10.571 --> 02:13.858
pillars. On the surface, it means highwalls,

02:13.858 --> 02:16.959
benches, the toe, and stockpile faces.

02:16.959 --> 02:19.534
The examiner looks for cracks, slabs,

02:19.534 --> 02:22.846
loose rock, drummy sounds, water, and anything

02:22.846 --> 02:24.846
that changed since last shift.

02:25.960 --> 02:28.923
Remember the Clover Bottom record. Four times,

02:28.923 --> 02:31.485
rock fell from the face or back while miners were

02:31.485 --> 02:34.235
scaling, checking for loose rock, or preparing

02:34.235 --> 02:37.335
a shot. Ground can change between shifts and even

02:37.335 --> 02:40.460
during a shift. The exam catches what it can,

02:40.460 --> 02:42.160
but you keep looking all day.

02:43.282 --> 02:46.782
Next, roads and edges. Is the berm still there,

02:46.782 --> 02:49.270
and is it at least mid-axle height of the largest

02:49.270 --> 02:51.870
equipment that usually travels that road?

02:51.870 --> 02:54.757
Did a truck clip it last shift? Is there a washout,

02:54.757 --> 02:57.920
a pothole, or a soft edge? Are dump points marked

02:57.920 --> 03:00.795
and protected? Are signs and traffic controls still

03:00.795 --> 03:01.870
where they belong?

03:02.980 --> 03:06.168
At the plant, the examiner looks at guarding first.

03:06.168 --> 03:09.130
Are the guards on the pulleys, shafts and couplings,

03:09.130 --> 03:11.943
and are they fastened? Are emergency stop cords

03:11.943 --> 03:14.668
and switches tight and working, or,

03:14.668 --> 03:17.280
where a belt is guarded by railings instead,

03:17.280 --> 03:20.343
are the railings in place? Are walkways and handrails

03:20.343 --> 03:23.530
solid, and are any openings in a deck or a catwalk

03:23.530 --> 03:25.155
covered or barricaded?

03:26.278 --> 03:28.741
Equipment checks are related, but they're a separate

03:28.741 --> 03:31.903
duty. Each operator still does the pre-shift check

03:31.903 --> 03:34.178
on their own machine and writes it on the Quarry

03:34.178 --> 03:36.553
Equipment Pre-Operational Inspection.

03:36.553 --> 03:39.478
Defects that affect safety are the most cited standard

03:39.478 --> 03:42.953
at all three of our sites. The workplace exam doesn't

03:42.953 --> 03:44.766
replace your pre-shift check.

03:45.893 --> 03:48.830
Then housekeeping. Spilled rock on a walkway,

03:48.830 --> 03:52.680
hoses across a path, oil on steps and ice in winter

03:52.680 --> 03:55.730
all trip people. Then electrical: damaged cords

03:55.730 --> 03:58.955
and cables, open panel doors, missing covers,

03:58.955 --> 04:02.243
missing insulating mats. And health hazards too,

04:02.243 --> 04:04.918
like heavy dust, a leaking spray bar,

04:04.918 --> 04:08.130
fumes in an enclosed area, or a fan that isn't

04:08.130 --> 04:08.805
running.

04:09.922 --> 04:12.960
The examiner doesn't just look for broken things.

04:12.960 --> 04:15.622
The examiner looks for conditions. A guard can

04:15.622 --> 04:18.360
be perfectly fine but sitting on the ground next

04:18.360 --> 04:21.347
to the pulley. A berm can be perfectly fine except

04:21.347 --> 04:24.460
for one gap. That one gap is the hazard.

04:25.588 --> 04:28.276
The shop is a working place too. The examiner looks

04:28.276 --> 04:32.026
at lifts, jacks and stands, at whether raised equipment

04:32.026 --> 04:34.701
is blocked, at air hoses and cords,

04:34.701 --> 04:37.563
at the hot-work area and what's stored near it,

04:37.563 --> 04:39.338
and at whether the fire extinguishers are where

04:39.338 --> 04:41.113
they belong and charged.

04:42.238 --> 04:45.026
Around drilling and blasting, the exam looks at

04:45.026 --> 04:48.526
the bench or face, the edge, the travel route for

04:48.526 --> 04:51.051
the drill, and whether the blast area is clear

04:51.051 --> 04:53.901
of anything that doesn't belong. Trainees don't

04:53.901 --> 04:57.126
make blasting decisions. You follow the signals,

04:57.126 --> 04:59.713
the boundaries and the all-clear from the authorized

04:59.713 --> 05:00.538
person.

05:03.872 --> 05:05.997
When the exam finds a condition that could affect

05:05.997 --> 05:09.510
safety or health, two things have to happen promptly.

05:09.510 --> 05:12.160
Miners in the affected area are told about it,

05:12.160 --> 05:14.360
and action to correct it is started.

05:14.360 --> 05:17.410
Correcting might mean scaling, rebuilding a berm,

05:17.410 --> 05:20.297
replacing a guard, cleaning up, or locking out

05:20.297 --> 05:22.147
a machine until it's repaired.

05:23.258 --> 05:25.396
If a hazard can't be fixed right away,

05:25.396 --> 05:27.958
it gets controlled. Hazardous ground that can't

05:27.958 --> 05:30.383
be corrected before work starts is posted with

05:30.383 --> 05:33.158
a warning against entry and barricaded until it's

05:33.158 --> 05:36.108
made safe. Other hazards that aren't obvious get

05:36.108 --> 05:39.271
barricaded or warning signs. Barricades and danger

05:39.271 --> 05:42.521
signs are cited at Clover Bottom. Here's your part:

05:42.521 --> 05:45.171
never cross a barricade or a danger sign,

05:45.171 --> 05:47.421
and never take one down unless you're the person

05:47.421 --> 05:48.646
authorized to.

05:49.772 --> 05:51.947
Some conditions are worse than a hazard.

05:51.947 --> 05:54.697
They're an imminent danger, where serious injury

05:54.697 --> 05:57.310
could happen before the condition is fixed.

05:57.310 --> 06:00.335
In that case, the examiner brings it to the operator's

06:00.335 --> 06:03.310
attention immediately, and everyone is withdrawn

06:03.310 --> 06:05.947
from the affected area. Only the people needed

06:05.947 --> 06:08.960
to fix it stay, and nobody else goes back in until

06:08.960 --> 06:10.285
the danger is gone.

06:13.614 --> 06:16.939
Every exam is recorded before the end of the shift.

06:16.939 --> 06:19.902
The record shows who did the exam, the date,

06:19.902 --> 06:22.689
every area examined, and a description of each

06:22.689 --> 06:26.039
condition found that may affect safety or health.

06:26.039 --> 06:28.689
When a condition gets fixed, the date of the corrective

06:28.689 --> 06:30.802
action is added to the record.

06:31.933 --> 06:34.433
The mine keeps those records for at least a year.

06:34.433 --> 06:37.183
MSHA inspectors and miners' representatives can

06:37.183 --> 06:40.095
look at them and ask for a copy. That's why a good

06:40.095 --> 06:43.108
record matters. It shows the hazard was seen,

06:43.108 --> 06:45.420
the people around it were told, and the hazard

06:45.420 --> 06:46.533
was dealt with.

06:47.658 --> 06:49.996
Get in the habit of asking about the exam for the

06:49.996 --> 06:52.671
place you're going to work. What was found today?

06:52.671 --> 06:55.033
Is anything still open? Is there a barricade you

06:55.033 --> 06:57.683
need to know about? If a condition is listed but

06:57.683 --> 07:00.633
not corrected yet, find out how it's being controlled

07:00.633 --> 07:03.296
before you go near it. Your supervisor will show

07:03.296 --> 07:05.546
you where the record is kept at your site.

07:06.660 --> 07:09.398
Compare two records. One just says the area looks

07:09.398 --> 07:12.210
okay. The other says the tail guard on a belt was

07:12.210 --> 07:15.298
off, the belt was locked out, miners at the plant

07:15.298 --> 07:17.685
were told, and the guard was replaced,

07:17.685 --> 07:20.235
with the date it was fixed. The second one tells

07:20.235 --> 07:22.623
the next shift exactly what happened.

07:22.623 --> 07:25.185
That's the kind of record the standard is after:

07:25.185 --> 07:27.785
who looked, where, what they found,

07:27.785 --> 07:29.135
and when it was corrected.

07:32.446 --> 07:36.059
An exam is a snapshot. A blast, a hard rain,

07:36.059 --> 07:39.009
a truck backing into a berm, or a rock that lets

07:39.009 --> 07:41.821
go after the exam can all create a new hazard in

07:41.821 --> 07:45.359
the middle of a shift. Examiners also miss things.

07:45.359 --> 07:47.859
They're human, and a working place is big.

07:48.989 --> 07:51.851
So the exam doesn't make a place safe forever.

07:51.851 --> 07:54.164
It's a starting point. Everyone who works there

07:54.164 --> 07:56.714
is part of the exam all shift long.

07:57.826 --> 07:59.839
When you find something the exam missed,

07:59.839 --> 08:02.664
do these four things. Stop, and don't work in the

08:02.664 --> 08:05.501
hazard. Warn anyone who could be exposed.

08:05.501 --> 08:07.676
Report it to your supervisor right away,

08:07.676 --> 08:09.876
and don't assume someone else already did.

08:09.876 --> 08:11.601
And don't try to fix it yourself unless you're

08:11.601 --> 08:14.126
trained and authorized for that fix.

08:15.244 --> 08:18.007
If it's loose ground, back out to supported,

08:18.007 --> 08:20.582
examined ground. If it's a missing guard,

08:20.582 --> 08:23.594
keep everyone away and report it. If it's a damaged

08:23.594 --> 08:26.844
cable, stay clear and report it. Your supervisor

08:26.844 --> 08:29.132
decides how it's corrected, and it should end up

08:29.132 --> 08:30.132
on the record.

08:31.284 --> 08:33.884
Here's how that sounds. A new miner walks up to

08:33.884 --> 08:36.634
a conveyor at the start of shift and sees the tail

08:36.634 --> 08:39.059
pulley guard leaning against the frame.

08:39.059 --> 08:41.534
He keeps everyone back and calls his lead on the

08:41.534 --> 08:44.221
radio: the tail guard on this belt is off,

08:44.221 --> 08:47.021
and the belt isn't safe to run. The lead says good

08:47.021 --> 08:49.896
catch, the belt stays down until the guard is back

08:49.896 --> 08:52.709
on, and the fix gets written up. That's a working

08:52.709 --> 08:53.834
exam system.

08:54.965 --> 08:58.140
To sum up. A competent person examines each working

08:58.140 --> 09:01.077
place every shift, before work starts.

09:01.077 --> 09:04.052
Miners are told about hazards, and fixes are started

09:04.052 --> 09:07.990
promptly. Imminent dangers mean everyone is withdrawn.

09:07.990 --> 09:10.615
The exam is recorded and kept for a year.

09:10.615 --> 09:12.852
And you keep looking all shift. When something's

09:12.852 --> 09:15.940
wrong, stop, warn, and report.

09:17.050 --> 09:20.213
Remember: your site's plans and procedures always

09:20.213 --> 09:21.712
override this video.
